(window.LADDER_CARDS["27a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["27a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"27a1.1","q":"What is a space of vectors, and what does the zero-stretch technique say?","a":"a set whose members add and stretch by numbers under the eight conditions; in every such space 0·v = 0, t·0 = 0 and (−1)·v = −v","w":["a set of arrows in the plane or in space only; 0·v = v and (−1)·v = −v","a set whose members add and multiply each other; 0·v = 0 and t·v = 0 only when t = 0","any set that holds a 0; t·0 = t and (−1)·v = v"],"why":"Lists, polynomials and solutions of y″ = −y are all spaces of vectors."},
 {"u":"27a2.1","q":"What does the inside-space technique say, and why need the eight conditions not be checked again in W?","a":"a part W of a space of vectors that holds 0 and is kept by adding and by stretching is itself a space of vectors; the conditions already hold for every vector of the larger space, so they hold for those in W","w":["any part of a space of vectors is a space of vectors; the conditions are inherited","a part W that holds 0 is a space of vectors; adding and stretching need not stay in W","a part W kept by adding is a space of vectors; the eight conditions must each be proved again in W"],"why":"Three checks stand in for eight."},
 {"u":"27a4.1","q":"What is wrong with “{(x, y) : x + y = 1} is a space of vectors”?","a":"it does not hold 0, and adding leaves it: (1, 0) + (0, 1) = (1, 1), whose entries add to 2","w":["it holds 0 but stretching leaves it","it is a line, and no line is a space of vectors","nothing; a line is always a space of vectors"],"why":"The line x + y = 0 through 0 is one; this one misses 0."},
 {"u":"27a3.1","q":"What did the proofs of 27a ask of the numbers that stretch, and what is a space of vectors over a field F? Why are the complex numbers over ℂ and the complex numbers over ℝ two spaces?","a":"only the nine techniques of adding and multiplying, with 0, 1, negatives and reciprocals, and never order; a space over F is a set that adds, and stretches by members of F, under the eight conditions. The set is the same but the stretching numbers differ: i is a stretch of 1 over ℂ and not over ℝ","w":["all twelve techniques, order included; a space over F is a set of lists of members of F. They are one space, since the set is the same","only that the numbers add; a space over F is a set that adds, and multiplies by members of F, under the nine techniques. They differ because ℝ has an order and ℂ has none","the nine techniques and a least upper bound; a space over F is a field that holds F. They differ because i has no square root in ℝ"],"why":"27a3: every line of the two proofs uses adding, multiplying, 0, 1, a negative or a reciprocal, and none uses order."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["27b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["27b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"27b1.1","q":"What does it mean for vectors to build V, and to be free, and what does the spare-vector technique say?","a":"they build V when every vector of V is a mix of them, and are free when only the mix with every number 0 gives 0; if a list with v₁ ≠ 0 is not free, some vⱼ is a mix of those before it, and dropping it loses no mix","w":["they build V when they lie in V, and are free when no two are equal; a list that is not free holds the zero vector","they build V when they are free, and are free when they build V; dropping any vector of a list loses no mix","they build V when some mix of them is 0, and are free when every mix is different from 0; the last vector of a list is always spare"],"why":"A spare vector adds nothing the others cannot make."},
 {"u":"27b2.1","q":"What does the exchange technique say, and why is the spare that goes out never one of the free vectors?","a":"if m vectors build V and k vectors of V are free, then k ≤ m; each free u is put in at the front, and the spare is a mix of the vectors before it, which cannot be a u among u’s because the u’s are free","w":["if m vectors build V and k are free, then k ≥ m; the spare is always the newest u","if m vectors build V then exactly m are free; the spare is chosen at will","if k free vectors lie in V then they build V; no spare is needed"],"why":"No free list is longer than a building list."},
 {"u":"27b3.1","q":"What is the dimension of a space, why is it well defined, and what does the grow-to-basis technique say?","a":"the number of vectors in a basis, a free list that builds the space; any two bases have the same number, by the exchange technique used both ways; and in dimension n every free list grows into a basis, and n free vectors already are one","w":["the largest entry in its lists; it is well defined by choice; any n vectors are a basis","the number of vectors in the space; it is well defined because the space is a set; every list can be shrunk to a basis of any size","the number of vectors in a basis; it depends on the basis chosen; n vectors that build the space need not be free"],"why":"Polynomials of degree at most n have dimension n + 1."},
 {"u":"27b7.1","q":"What is wrong with “each pair of the list is free, so the list is free”?","a":"freedom is a demand on the whole list: every pair of (1, 0), (0, 1), (1, 1) is free, and yet the third is the sum of the first two","w":["nothing; a list is free exactly when its pairs are","the pairs must be checked in order, first with second, second with third","it fails only for lists that hold the zero vector"],"why":"Three vectors in a space of dimension 2 are never free."},
 {"u":"27b4.1","q":"What do two spaces inside a space build together, and what does the two-spaces count technique say? When is every vector of U + W a sum u + w in exactly one way?","a":"all the sums u + w, which is a space; dim(U + W) + dim(U ∩ W) = dim U + dim W; exactly when U and W share only 0","w":["their union, which is a space; dim(U + W) = dim U + dim W always; exactly when U and W have the same dimension","all the sums u + w, which is a space; dim(U + W) = dim U · dim W; exactly when U is inside W","all the sums u + w, which need not be a space; dim(U + W) + dim(U ∩ W) = dim V; always"],"why":"27b4: a basis of what they share grows to a basis of each, and the three lists together are a basis of U + W."},
 {"u":"27b5.1","q":"What does the one-list technique say, and which condition on a basis gives “exactly one list”? How do the numbers of a vector in a second basis come from its numbers in the first?","a":"every vector is x₁v₁ + ⋯ + xₙvₙ for exactly one list, and lists add and stretch as the vectors do; freedom gives “only one”. Write each old basis vector in the new basis, and the new numbers are yᵢ = pᵢ₁x₁ + ⋯ + pᵢₙxₙ","w":["every vector has at least one list; building gives “only one”. The new numbers are the old numbers in the opposite order","every vector is x₁v₁ + ⋯ + xₙvₙ for exactly one list; building gives “only one”. The numbers of a vector are the same in every basis","every list names at most one vector; freedom gives “at least one”. Write each new basis vector in the old basis, and the new numbers are yᵢ = xᵢ/pᵢᵢ"],"why":"27b5: two lists for one vector differ by a mix equal to 0, and a free list allows only the mix with every number 0."},
 {"u":"27b6.1","q":"Why does no finite list of polynomials build all the polynomials? Which techniques of 27b need a finite list that builds the space?","a":"every mix of a finite list has degree at most the greatest degree d in the list, so x to the d + 1 is not a mix; the same-size and grow-to-basis techniques, which begin from a finite basis","w":["because polynomials cannot be added; the spare-vector technique only","because the powers 1, x, x², … are not free; none of them needs a finite list","every mix of a finite list has degree exactly d, so the constants are not mixes; the exchange technique fails for polynomials"],"why":"27b6: adding and stretching never raise the degree, and a finite list has a greatest degree."});
